\parbold{Strategic classification} As in strategic classification~\cite{Hardt:2016:SC,bruckner2012static,dong2018strategic}, we consider the challenge an institution faces when deploying a classifier that applicants can game; a naive classifier that does not factor in the resulting distribution shifts would be inaccurate. Unlike strategic classification, which considers the manipulation of observable features to be unproductive from the institution's perspective, our model of efforts stipulates that the school prefers higher effort levels as they give rise to higher scores.  We follow the line of work considering the design of reward functions that further incentivize agent effort on productive tasks~\cite{kleinberg18investeffort, miller2020strategic,bechavod2021gaming,shavit2020causal}. We in particular draw inspiration from and compare to several recent directions in the strategic classification literature: with multiple agents \citep{haghtalab2020maximizing,alon2020multiagent}, when a classifier may be random \citep{braverman2020role}, and with fairness concerns \citep{hu2019disparate,Milli2019social}. Like other strategic classification work, our work is further related to the prior economics literature on contract design, elaborated in \Cref{sec:multi-dim}. % \todo{other contract design}

%~\citet{haghtalab2020maximizing} and~\citet{alon2020multiagent} both study multi-agent strategic classification where the principal's goal is to incentivize agents to improve their features in a productive manner (as opposed to, e.g., cheating); in our setting, the different agents may have different initial features and cost functions, but also impose competitive pressures on each other. \citet{hu2019disparate} and \citet{Milli2019social} consider the (potentially heterogeneous) cost of strategic classification on applicants, as in equilibrium (due to the classifier's adjustment) they must exert extra effort to be admitted. In our model with disparate costs, a student from the disadvantaged group similarly must exert a greater effort to be admitted than a student from the advantaged group, and this effort differential worsens with increased competition. Finally, \citet{braverman2020role} study the role of randomized classifiers and noisy features in strategic classification, showing that a randomized classifier can prevent  undesirable strategic behavior {by an applicant}; they further show that \textit{after} applicants have responded to a randomized classifier, randomization is not a best response for the institution. It is also shown that noisy features can reduce disparate impact when there are two subpopulations with different costs of manipulation. In our setting, randomization (after applicants have been ranked) plays three roles: to prevent strategic behavior at the cost of other, less measurable beneficial features, to lessen competitive pressure, and to increase fairness in the presence of disparate costs. %In our setting, we find that using a randomized decision policy could be justified by social welfare objectives or when there is a unmeasurable attribute.


