\begin{restatable}[Rank preservation]{proposition}{lemassortative}
	\label{lem:rankpreservedindex}
In every equilibrium, $ \lambda(\theta_\post(\theta_\pre))=\lambda(\theta_\pre)$, up to %changes with
{sets of} measure 0. %, up to tie-breaking for applicants with identical post-effort skills.
\end{restatable}

As shown in the next section, rank preservation simplifies substantially the evaluation of various utility functions, as the post-effort rankings of applicants are fixed and known.

The result follows from the shared cost function $\cost$ being convex and increasing on $[e_0, \infty)$: if an applicant pre-effort rank $\theta$ finds it optimal to achieve post-effort value $v$, then each applicant with pre-effort rank $\bar\theta > \theta$ finds it optimal to reach a post-effort value $\bar v\ge v$. We note that this result (and all our subsequent results) hold for any tie-breaking function %$\gamma$,
{$\Gamma$,}
as any set of post-effort values in which tie-breaking occurs across two or more reward bands $\psi$ cannot constitute an equilibrium. %While in this work we assume a step-wise increasing function $\lambda$ with $K$ levels, the result holds generally for non-decreasing $\lambda$.

